WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.177 --> 00:00:04.350
Software developers, we are not known
for our rocking style, are we?

00:00:04.988 --> 00:00:06.627
Or are we?

00:00:06.875 --> 00:00:09.547
Today I'll show you how I use ML
to make me trendier,

00:00:09.547 --> 00:00:11.526
taking inspiration from influencers.

00:00:11.783 --> 00:00:14.111
I'm going to build an app
that analyzes my closet,

00:00:14.111 --> 00:00:16.192
and pictures of fashionable influencers,

00:00:16.192 --> 00:00:18.638
and then makes me outfit recommendations.

00:00:18.638 --> 00:00:20.178
If I'm missing an important piece,

00:00:20.178 --> 00:00:22.040
it'll also recommend me
products to buy.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.233
Let me show you how.

00:00:23.233 --> 00:00:26.228
♪ [music] ♪

00:00:28.359 --> 00:00:29.658
So the way this is going to work

00:00:29.658 --> 00:00:32.702
is I'm going to grab a bunch
of fashionable inspiration pictures

00:00:32.702 --> 00:00:33.572
from social media,

00:00:33.572 --> 00:00:35.796
and I'll take a bunch of pictures
of my wardrobe,

00:00:35.796 --> 00:00:37.670
and I'll try to match the pieces
that I already own

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.256
to those fashion pictures
using Machine Learning,

00:00:40.256 --> 00:00:42.157
which will help me put together outfits.

00:00:42.157 --> 00:00:44.108
I'll wrap this whole thing
in a Firebase app

00:00:44.108 --> 00:00:46.770
so I can scroll through outfit
recommendations on my phone.

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.390
All right. Let's build this thing.

00:00:48.390 --> 00:00:50.989
First, I needed to catalog
my entire wardrobe.

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:53.124
I thought I could just grab
a few pictures of me

00:00:53.124 --> 00:00:54.737
wearing clothing in everyday life

00:00:54.737 --> 00:00:57.363
and then use computer vision 
to pick up the items.

00:00:57.363 --> 00:01:00.370
To do this I used a feature
of the Google Cloud Vision API

00:01:00.370 --> 00:01:03.558
called Object Detection,
which identifies objects in photos

00:01:03.558 --> 00:01:06.201
and then draws a little bounding box
around them.

00:01:06.201 --> 00:01:08.317
As you can see, it kind of worked.

00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:12.182
It identified my top, shorts,
shoes, stuff like that.

00:01:12.182 --> 00:01:14.352
But since I'm going to be
matching my clothing pictures

00:01:14.352 --> 00:01:15.690
to inspiration pictures,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.541
I realized that the quality
of my pictures had to be really high.

00:01:18.541 --> 00:01:21.702
They couldn't be at weird lighting angles
or anything like that.

00:01:21.983 --> 00:01:23.484
So I accepted the sad fate

00:01:23.484 --> 00:01:27.315
that I was going to have to take a picture
of every single item in my closet,

00:01:27.315 --> 00:01:30.388
which I did painstakingly
over a couple of days,

00:01:30.648 --> 00:01:32.461
dressing and undressing my mannequin

00:01:32.461 --> 00:01:34.621
and taking pictures
of all my different shoes.

00:01:34.621 --> 00:01:36.039
Next, I needed to find some people

00:01:36.039 --> 00:01:38.032
who actually knew something
about looking trendy,

00:01:38.032 --> 00:01:40.135
and bonus points
if they also worked in tech.

00:01:40.135 --> 00:01:42.321
So I reached out to Laura Medalia.

00:01:42.321 --> 00:01:43.767
Laura is a developer who posts

00:01:43.767 --> 00:01:46.428
lots of fashionable outfit picks
of herself on Instagram,

00:01:46.428 --> 00:01:48.882
where she is @codergirl_,
and from head to toe,

00:01:48.882 --> 00:01:51.390
which is important because
we need to capture those shoes.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.636
With her permission,
I downloaded a bunch of her pictures

00:01:53.636 --> 00:01:55.380
and uploaded them to Cloud Storage.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.041
Again, the goal here is to take
a bunch of Laura's pictures

00:01:58.041 --> 00:01:59.590
and then pictures from my own closet,

00:01:59.590 --> 00:02:02.032
and see which of her outfits
I can kind of recreate.

00:02:02.032 --> 00:02:04.335
Now, challenge one was
that not all Laura's pictures

00:02:04.335 --> 00:02:05.454
are actually of clothing,

00:02:05.454 --> 00:02:07.811
so, first, I had to eliminate those
and select only the ones

00:02:07.811 --> 00:02:09.215
where she is in an outfit.

00:02:09.215 --> 00:02:11.264
For that, I used a feature
of the Vision API

00:02:11.264 --> 00:02:13.087
that does image classification.

00:02:13.087 --> 00:02:16.092
This feature returns a bunch 
of tags describing what in general

00:02:16.092 --> 00:02:17.346
is going on in an image,

00:02:17.346 --> 00:02:19.408
and one of the tags it returns is <i>Fashion.</i>

00:02:19.408 --> 00:02:21.852
I sorted through all
of Laura's pictures and only kept ones

00:02:21.852 --> 00:02:24.763
that had a person in them,
and they were also labeled <i>Fashion.</i>

00:02:24.763 --> 00:02:27.209
And with that, I had
my inspiration image dataset.

00:02:27.752 --> 00:02:31.041
Now, the next step is to implement
the feature that looks at my closet

00:02:31.041 --> 00:02:33.321
and the inspiration picture
and matches them.

00:02:33.631 --> 00:02:35.529
For that, I'm going to use
yet another feature

00:02:35.529 --> 00:02:37.965
of Google Vision API
called Product Search.

00:02:37.965 --> 00:02:39.607
If you're ever scrolling through a store,

00:02:39.607 --> 00:02:41.956
and you're looking at a product,
and you see similar items,

00:02:41.956 --> 00:02:42.923
that's the functionality

00:02:42.923 --> 00:02:45.545
that the Google Vision
Product Search feature gives you.

00:02:45.545 --> 00:02:47.528
The next step is to create a product set.

00:02:47.528 --> 00:02:50.587
For this, you want to use
the <i>pyvisionproductsearch</i> library.

00:02:50.587 --> 00:02:53.665
The first step is to create
a <i>ProductSearch</i> client

00:02:53.665 --> 00:02:56.003
that connects to the Google Cloud backend,

00:02:56.337 --> 00:02:58.035
and next, you'll create a <i>productSet</i>

00:02:58.035 --> 00:03:00.576
where you can hold
all of your clothing items.

00:03:00.576 --> 00:03:02.565
I called mine "dales_closet."

00:03:02.965 --> 00:03:04.391
Next, you want to go to the spot

00:03:04.391 --> 00:03:06.409
where all of your clothing items
are stored.

00:03:06.409 --> 00:03:09.010
As you can see,
I've got all of mine in a directory,

00:03:09.010 --> 00:03:11.806
and if I want to add something
like this black bomber jacket,

00:03:11.806 --> 00:03:14.063
in the code I create a new <i>product,</i>

00:03:14.063 --> 00:03:17.152
I name it, I give it the category <i>apparel,</i>

00:03:17.152 --> 00:03:20.381
and then I add any labels that I might
want to later go back and filter by,

00:03:20.381 --> 00:03:22.226
so just show me jackets.

00:03:22.613 --> 00:03:24.244
And the next step
is to teach the model

00:03:24.244 --> 00:03:26.160
what this bomber jacket looks like.

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:29.343
For that, you want to add paths
to all of the different files

00:03:29.343 --> 00:03:30.748
as reference images.

00:03:31.684 --> 00:03:33.520
It's better if you can take pictures
from different angles

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.002
with different lighting conditions,

00:03:35.002 --> 00:03:37.497
because it will help
the model generalize better.

00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:39.505
To make this a little bit easier for you,

00:03:39.505 --> 00:03:42.976
I wrote a script in Python
so that if you organize your images

00:03:42.976 --> 00:03:44.664
into a directory structure,

00:03:44.664 --> 00:03:46.286
then you can just run this script,

00:03:46.286 --> 00:03:48.498
and it will automatically create
a product set for you.

00:03:48.498 --> 00:03:50.690
Make sure you check out
the blog post to see how.

00:03:50.690 --> 00:03:52.380
Once you're done creating
your product set,

00:03:52.380 --> 00:03:55.570
it'll take about 30 minutes for the API
to actually index those images.

00:03:55.570 --> 00:03:57.010
So just hang tight.

00:03:57.010 --> 00:03:59.828
When it's finished, we get to actually
make outfits with ML.

00:04:00.021 --> 00:04:02.782
To do that, we'll call the <i>Search</i> function
on the Product Search API

00:04:02.782 --> 00:04:04.781
and pass it a path to an image file.

00:04:05.039 --> 00:04:07.862
First, it analyzes that image
and identifies any clothing it finds,

00:04:07.862 --> 00:04:10.263
then it looks through your closet
and finds the best matches.

00:04:10.263 --> 00:04:12.034
And now, here's where it gets interesting.

00:04:12.034 --> 00:04:14.613
Because even though
the API returns back similar items,

00:04:14.613 --> 00:04:17.119
what we really want
are outfit recommendations.

00:04:17.288 --> 00:04:20.019
An outfit like has only one pair of shoes,

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:23.315
and you can't be wearing a skirt
and pants at the same time, probably.

00:04:23.405 --> 00:04:25.455
So we need to do
a little bit of finagling.

00:04:25.455 --> 00:04:27.612
And that's kind of par for the course
with ML models.

00:04:27.612 --> 00:04:29.936
They usually don't give you back
exactly what you want,

00:04:29.936 --> 00:04:32.511
so you have to use your expertise
and domain knowledge

00:04:32.511 --> 00:04:34.627
to make sure the predictions make sense.

00:04:34.627 --> 00:04:36.842
So I'm going to roll my
own outfit algorithm

00:04:36.842 --> 00:04:40.318
that defines rules for what pieces
of clothing can be in an outfit.

00:04:40.318 --> 00:04:42.059
And it'll take
all of the confidence scores

00:04:42.059 --> 00:04:44.946
returned by the API, and generate
one new quality score

00:04:44.946 --> 00:04:46.510
for the entire outfit.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.139
I did all of my outfit-making logic
in a Python script,

00:04:49.139 --> 00:04:52.527
which wrote out all
of my outfit recommendations to Firestore.

00:04:52.801 --> 00:04:54.278
Then I built a little frontend app

00:04:54.278 --> 00:04:57.644
that pulls in all the recommendations,
sorted in score order.

00:04:57.738 --> 00:04:59.527
Let's take a look at how I did.

00:04:59.527 --> 00:05:02.528
♪ [music] ♪

00:05:04.412 --> 00:05:06.487
If you want all the details
on how I built this app,

00:05:06.487 --> 00:05:08.872
make sure you check out
the blog post link below.

00:05:08.872 --> 00:05:10.647
And follow codergirl_ on Instagram

00:05:10.647 --> 00:05:13.276
to get your own neat techy outfit inspo.

00:05:13.570 --> 00:05:14.942
See you next time.

00:05:14.942 --> 00:05:17.947
♪ [music] ♪

